#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Carry a user's Alacritty setup over to Ghostty, once, at their first graphical login.

Rime's default terminal is Ghostty; Alacritty stays installed. This runs at every graphical login (a user oneshot)
and does two things:

  1. Seeds Rime's Ghostty theme, ~/.config/ghostty/themes/rime-os, when it is missing. The theme is what makes
     Ghostty look like Rime's Alacritty: same font, size, colours, padding and cursor. Rime Shell replaces this
     file with wallpaper colours (its matugen template); a seeded copy starts with FALLBACK_MARK so the Shell knows
     it may render over it, and so this script may refresh it when Rime's shipped copy changes. A theme without
     that mark is the Shell's (or the user's) and is never touched.
  2. Once per user (stamp: ~/.local/state/rime/terminal-migrated), writes ~/.config/ghostty/config.ghostty:
     `theme = rime-os` plus every Alacritty setting that differs from Alacritty's defaults and has a Ghostty
     equivalent. A report of what was carried over and what was not goes to
     ~/.local/state/rime/terminal-migration.txt.

What it never does: edit or replace a Ghostty config that already has settings (it says so in the report and
stops), change any Alacritty file, or fail the login (every error is logged and the exit status is 0).

`--convert FILE` prints the Ghostty lines and the report for one Alacritty file without writing anything (tests).
"""

